The old saying : "favor composition over inheritance!"
                  inheritance is not bad, but sometime composition is better solution.


now we have CloudStream as parent and
Encrypt & Compress class as children,
but we can convert this "vertical" relationship (hierarchy by inheritance)
to some "horizontal" relationship (like Chain of Responsibility pattern)
we can introduce an Interface like "Stream"              (it's like handler class in Chain of Responsibility)
and use a field based on "Stream" type to link classes   (it's like next filed in Chain of Responsibility)

Note: base class (CloudStream) has not the "Stream" field
      it's the core of chain!
      it's like we start from end of chain in Chain of Responsibility

Note: the core idea is almost same,
      but the purpose is different
      Decorator wraps a single "object"
      Chain of Responsibility operates on a "request"
      In Decorator, the final output is the composition of all layers.
      In Chain of Responsibility, only one handler may respond.
      In Decorator, the focus is on composing behaviors.
      In Chain of Responsibility, the focus is on passing responsibility.
      In Decorator order is not the matter
      In Chain of Responsibility order is matter!

Note: we use composition (inject Stream type)
      to create chain.
      we create a chain to customize it in each step
      and at last in base class we call the actual code (like write() on
                                                         CloudStream class)


